### Page 6 of 7

```text
ATTACHMENT A
SERVICES TO BE PROVIDED BY YOUTH ALTERNATIVES TO THE LARAMIE
COUNTY SHERIFF’S DEPARTMENT AT THE JUVENILE SERVICE CENTER
The proposed agreement between Laramie County and the Office of Youth Alternatives for the
provision of screening, counseling and supportive services to youth refelTed to the Laramie
County Juvenile Services Center
— Youth Crisis Center (JSC-YCC).
The services would include:
1.
24/7/3 65 availability for crisis support;
2.
Phone assessment;
3.
Crisis meeting assessment;
4.
Support services for youth and families redirected from the JSC-YCC;
5.
Family counseling and support for families in crisis;
6.
Individual and family goal development for admitted youth;
7.
Discharge planning;
8.
Conduct discharge meetings;
9.
Provide follow-up services for discharged youth, including referral to service providers if
necessary; and
10.
Provide on-going family counseling or referral to appropriate service providers.
To provide these services, Youth Alternatives will use its current on-call system. Coverage
would be provided by a family counselor hired to support these services and by other Youth
Alternatives’ staff.
Youth Alternatives will be responsible for assuring that all services are provided by practitioners
with appropriate licensing or under licensed supervision.
When Youth Alternatives receives a call for services, the on-call staff will contact the JSC-YCC
to determine eligibility and bed
space availability.
Those situations involving physical
altercations may not meet eligibility requirements for the Youth Crisis Center, but should still be
discussed, as there may be mitigating factors. Parent or youth referrals in which there is a clear
and imminent danger of physical altercation law enforcement should be contacted; if not already
present.
The procedures to be used to refer juveniles to the JSC-YCC and to other community services
will be jointly agreed upon by Youth Alternatives and the JSC-YCC. The procedures are broadly
described in “Admission Flow for Referrals to Juvenile Services Crisis Shelter” drafted by Youth
Alternatives and approved by the Laramie County Juvenile Services Center.
Page 6 of 7
